### Reliable Checkout & Rewards Service — executable API examples
###
### Run top-to-bottom against a FRESHLY SEEDED instance (N=5, X=10 defaults):
###   cd backend && rm -f app.db* && python -m app.seed && uvicorn app.main:app --port 8000
###
### This file is a single continuous script: later requests depend on state
### created by earlier ones (e.g. reaching 5 successful orders before the
### milestone coupon can be generated). Every request that captures a value
### for later use is named with "# @name" and referenced as
### {{name.response.body.$.field}} — this is the chaining syntax used by the
### "REST Client" VS Code extension (humao.rest-client) and IntelliJ's HTTP
### client. If your tool doesn't support chaining, just read each response as
### you go and paste the id/code into the next request manually — the file
### still works as a step-by-step reference either way.
###
### Verified end-to-end against the running backend before being committed:
### every status code and error code below was captured from a real run,
### not hand-typed.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000

### ============================================================
### 1. HAPPY PATH — create cart, add item, view cart, checkout, get order
### ============================================================

# Create an empty cart. -> 201, empty items, subtotal "0.00"
# @name cart1
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

# Add 2x Widget (product_id=1, ₹199.00 each) to the cart. -> 201, subtotal "398.00"
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart1.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 1,
  "quantity": 2
}

###

# View the cart — always shows LIVE current prices, never a stale snapshot. -> 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart1.response.body.$.cart_id}}

###

# Checkout with no coupon. First success on this cart -> 201, total == subtotal.
# @name cart1Checkout
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart1.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# Fetch the order back. Demonstrates the order retains its own price/name
# snapshot independent of the live product catalog. -> 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/{{cart1Checkout.response.body.$.order_id}}

### ============================================================
### 2. ERROR CASES — one request per error code in errors.py
### ============================================================

# CART_NOT_FOUND (404) — a cart id that was never created (or already GC'd).
GET {{baseUrl}}/carts/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

###

# @name cart2
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

# PRODUCT_NOT_FOUND (404) — product_id doesn't exist.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart2.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 999999,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# INVALID_QUANTITY (400) — quantity <= 0. Distinct from VALIDATION_ERROR (422):
# this is a business-rule rejection, not a schema-shape rejection.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart2.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 1,
  "quantity": 0
}

###

# CART_ITEM_NOT_FOUND (404) — item_id doesn't exist on this cart.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart2.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items/999999
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# CART_ALREADY_CHECKED_OUT (409) — mutating cart1, which was already checked
# out in section 1. Any cart mutation (add/update/remove item) after checkout
# gets this, not a generic error.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart1.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 2,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# @name cart3
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

# CART_EMPTY (409) — checkout attempted on a cart with zero items.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart3.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# @name cart4
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

# Doohickey (product_id=4) is seeded with inventory=3 specifically to make
# this demonstrable. Adding 10 succeeds (cart mutation doesn't check stock)...
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart4.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 4,
  "quantity": 10
}

###

# ...but INSUFFICIENT_INVENTORY (409) fires at checkout, with the actual
# available count in error.details.available. Inventory is left untouched
# (verify with GET /products/4 — still shows inventory: 3).
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart4.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# ORDER_NOT_FOUND (404) — an order id that doesn't exist.
GET {{baseUrl}}/orders/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

### ============================================================
### 3. MILESTONE — not-yet-eligible before enough orders exist
### ============================================================

# MILESTONE_NOT_YET_ELIGIBLE (409) — only 1 successful order exists so far
# (from section 1), but N=5 are required for milestone #1. error.details
# shows { current_orders, required_orders, next_milestone }.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/coupons/generate

### ============================================================
### 4. IDEMPOTENCY — dual layer: Idempotency-Key header + cart-state
### ============================================================

# @name cart5
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart5.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# First checkout with an Idempotency-Key. -> 201 (a new order is created).
# @name cart5Checkout
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart5.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: demo-key-1

{}

###

# Replaying the SAME Idempotency-Key against the SAME cart returns the
# stored response verbatim — still 201 (not re-executed), byte-for-byte
# identical body to the request above, and inventory is decremented only
# once (verify with GET /products/3).
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart5.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: demo-key-1

{}

###

# Retrying with NO Idempotency-Key against an already-CHECKED_OUT cart hits
# the other idempotency layer (cart-state): it replays the same order but
# with 200, not 201, since no new resource was created by this call.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart5.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# @name cart6
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart6.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_CONFLICT (409) — reusing "demo-key-1" for a DIFFERENT cart
# than the one it was first used for. This is client misuse (reusing a key
# across unrelated requests), not a retry, so cart6 is untouched — it stays
# OPEN and this call creates no order.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart6.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json
Idempotency-Key: demo-key-1

{}

### ============================================================
### 5. ADMIN FLOW — reach the milestone, generate, redeem, view the report
### ============================================================

# Three more quick orders to bring the running total to 5 successful orders
# (1 from section 1 + 1 from section 4's idempotency demo + these 3).

# @name filler1
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{filler1.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{filler1.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# @name filler2
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{filler2.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{filler2.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# @name filler3
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{filler3.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{filler3.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{}

###

# Now at exactly 5 successful orders -> milestone #1 is reached. -> 201,
# a coupon like "SAVE10-XXXXXX" with discount_percent=10 (X=10 default).
# @name coupon
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/coupons/generate

###

# Calling generate again IMMEDIATELY -> MILESTONE_NOT_YET_ELIGIBLE (409)
# again, this time for milestone #2 (needs 10 total orders, only 5 exist).
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/coupons/generate

###

# Admin report: pure read, no side effects. Calling it again with no new
# orders in between returns byte-for-byte the same body (see
# test_report_stable_across_repeated_calls). net_revenue always reconciles
# to gross_revenue - total_discount.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/report

###

# COUPON_NOT_FOUND (400) — a coupon code that was never generated.
# @name cart7
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart7.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart7.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "coupon_code": "NOPE-000000"
}

###

# @name cart8
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart8.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# Redeem the real coupon from above -> 201, discount = 10% of the subtotal,
# coupon_code echoed back in the response.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart8.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "coupon_code": "{{coupon.response.body.$.code}}"
}

###

# @name cart9
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts

###

POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart9.response.body.$.cart_id}}/items
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "product_id": 3,
  "quantity": 1
}

###

# COUPON_ALREADY_REDEEMED (409) — same coupon code, a different cart, after
# it was already spent above. Note this is a DIFFERENT error from
# COUPON_NOT_FOUND: the code is real, it's just gone.
POST {{baseUrl}}/carts/{{cart9.response.body.$.cart_id}}/checkout
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "coupon_code": "{{coupon.response.body.$.code}}"
}
